window.GUIDE_CONTENT=Object.assign(window.GUIDE_CONTENT||{},
{
  "archive-for-dummies": {
    "body": "\n<h2 id=\"start\">so your favorite bot disappeared. now what?</h2>\n<p>first, don't delete your old chat or assume everything's gone just because the bot stopped showing up in search. the Archive might still have its public definition, an older version, or at least its name and creator. your own conversation might help fill the gaps too.</p>\n<p>this is the beginner walkthrough, based on the Archive's <a href=\"https://spicychatarchive.drache.uk/guide/\">Archive for Dummies</a> page. if you're looking for the other features, stats and integrations, use our <a href=\"/archive/\">full Archive guide</a>.</p>\n<nav class=\"recovery-paths\" aria-label=\"Choose your starting point\">\n<a href=\"#bot-id\"><strong>i still have the chat or link</strong><span>find its exact bot ID</span></a>\n<a href=\"#without-id\"><strong>i only remember the name or picture</strong><span>search older public records</span></a>\n<a href=\"#recovery-lab\"><strong>i found the Archive record</strong><span>start rebuilding in Recovery Lab</span></a>\n<a href=\"#save-chat\"><strong>my conversation still opens</strong><span>save it before doing anything else</span></a>\n</nav>\n<h2 id=\"limits\">the important part: what can actually be restored?</h2>\n<div class=\"guide-callout\"><strong>the Archive cannot undelete the original bot.</strong> it can't restore the original bot ID, connect the old chat to a new bot, retrieve private definitions, contact creators for you or get around platform restrictions. only the creator or SpicyChat can decide whether an original bot can return.</div>\n<p>Recovery Lab helps you create a <strong>separate new copy</strong> using public information that was actually saved, plus evidence you already have permission to use. and a bot that's unavailable might be private, unlisted or temporarily missing rather than permanently deleted.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"before-start\">before you start: keep these things if you have them</h2>\n<p>you don't need all of these. having even one useful piece of information can make the search easier. but i'd keep the originals rather than rewriting everything from memory.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>public bot link or UUID:</strong> the most reliable way to identify which character you're talking about.</li>\n<li><strong>character name, full title and creator:</strong> especially the creator's @handle if you remember it.</li>\n<li><strong>screenshots:</strong> avatar, title, Greeting and other public fields; screenshots are useful clues but aren't a machine-readable full backup.</li>\n<li><strong>existing conversation:</strong> the old chat, a QoL or S.AI Toolkit export, or a manually copied transcript.</li>\n<li><strong>older saved cards:</strong> if you created the bot, a real JSON/PNG character card or your own Lorebook backup may be better than a reconstruction.</li>\n<li><strong>dates and version clues:</strong> when you used the bot, when it changed, and which version felt familiar.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>make a separate folder for the files, preferably on your own device. keep the unedited export in there even if you work on a cleaned copy later. it's much easier to compare evidence when the original files haven't been overwritten.</p>\n<div class=\"example-box\"><pre>Example local folder:\nMissing-Bot-Recovery/\n  bot-link.txt\n  screenshot-of-listing.png\n  original-chat-export.json\n  archive-evidence-notes.txt\n  recovery-package.json\n\nThese are suggested filenames, not files Recovery Lab creates automatically.</pre></div>\n<p>if a file contains private roleplay, treat it like a private conversation, not like something you should upload to a public Discord thread.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"bot-id\">1. find the exact bot ID</h2>\n<p>if you've still got a link, this is the easiest part. the bot ID is a UUID: letters, numbers and dashes. it isn't the character's name or the ID of your individual conversation.</p>\n<ol><li>open the character's profile or your existing conversation.</li><li>copy the browser URL, or use Share / Copy link if that works.</li><li>in a profile URL, find the UUID after <code>/chatbot/</code>. in a chat URL, use the <strong>first UUID after /chat/</strong>—the next one is the conversation ID.</li><li>paste the bot ID or its public profile link into <a href=\"https://spicychatarchive.drache.uk/recovery/\">Recovery Lab</a> or the Archive's search pages.</li></ol>\n<div class=\"example-box\"><pre>Profile: spicychat.ai/chatbot/11111111-2222-4333-8444-555555555555\nChat:    spicychat.ai/chat/11111111-2222-4333-8444-555555555555/aaaaaaaa-bbbb-4ccc-8ddd-eeeeeeeeeeee\n\nBOT ID:          11111111-2222-4333-8444-555555555555\nCONVERSATION ID: aaaaaaaa-bbbb-4ccc-8ddd-eeeeeeeeeeee</pre></div>\n<p>these are fake IDs for the example. language-prefixed links such as <code>/ru/chatbot/</code> work similarly. even when a chat header is blank, its URL might still contain the bot ID.</p>\n<h3>on a phone</h3>\n<p>try the official app's Share option. if that fails, open SpicyChat in your mobile browser, check your old conversation URL or look through bookmarks and browser history.</p>\n<p>when asking someone for help, send the <strong>public bot ID or profile URL</strong>, not the full private conversation URL. and never share cookies, session tokens or passwords.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"url-troubleshooting\">what if the link looks weird or doesn't open?</h2>\n<p>you don't need the page to load successfully to read a UUID from an old URL. the Archive's Recovery Lab looks for a UUID inside the text you paste, so a saved link can still be useful even if the original bot disappeared.</p>\n<p>the easiest check is to count the identifiers: a normal bot-profile URL has one bot ID, while a chat URL can have the bot ID followed by a different conversation ID. <strong>use the bot one</strong>. don't confuse it with a username, a numeric profile ID or a link-shortener address.</p>\n<p>if the link comes from a screenshot, don't guess missing UUID characters. try browser history, a bookmark, an old message where you shared the bot, or your existing chat before switching to name-only search.</p>\n<p>Recovery Lab needs a valid UUID for direct loading. if you only have a name, start with <a href=\"https://spicychatarchive.drache.uk/search/\">Archive Search</a> or <a href=\"https://spicychatarchive.drache.uk/bots/\">Public bots</a> and obtain the ID from the matching result first.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"without-id\">2. no ID? search what you still remember</h2>\n<p>start with the bot's name, title or creator's username. a screenshot with readable text can help, but the Archive doesn't promise reverse-image search.</p>\n<ol><li>try <a href=\"https://spicychatarchive.drache.uk/search/\">Archive Search</a> with the title or a distinctive phrase.</li><li>check <a href=\"https://spicychatarchive.drache.uk/deleted/\">Deleted bots</a> and <a href=\"https://spicychatarchive.drache.uk/bots/\">Public bots</a>—it might still be available.</li><li>search the creator's name and shorter or alternative spellings.</li><li>clear any tag filters or locally hidden-bot filters, then compare the result's creator and picture.</li></ol>\n<p>the Archive might only have an old search reference, or might never have collected the bot at all. <strong>no result doesn't prove the bot never existed.</strong></p>\n\n<h2 id=\"search-plan\">searching without an ID: a practical order</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>What you remember</th><th>Where i'd start</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>exact bot ID</td><td>Recovery Lab or a direct Archive bot record</td></tr>\n<tr><td>character name and title</td><td>Archive Search, then compare public and deleted listings</td></tr>\n<tr><td>creator's @username</td><td>Public bots / historical search narrowed by creator</td></tr>\n<tr><td>a distinctive line from the Greeting</td><td>Archive Search for that memorable phrase</td></tr>\n<tr><td>only a screenshot</td><td>visible title, username, spelling and avatar comparison</td></tr>\n<tr><td>no details beyond a vague premise</td><td>try related terms, but accept that there may not be enough evidence to identify it safely</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>make sure you're looking at the correct character before copying anything. common names, reused avatars and similar fan characters can produce false matches.</p>\n<p>another common mistake is filtering the search so heavily that a perfectly good result disappears. clear your optional tags, creator or locally hidden-card filters and try again before concluding the Archive missed it.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"record\">3. check what the Archive actually saved</h2>\n<p>open the matching record. compare its name, creator, date and any historical versions before copying text. a newer version might be very different from the character you remember.</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Archive result</th><th>what it means</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Saved record</td><td>some real public fields were captured, but it might still be incomplete</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Search entry only</td><td>there might be a name or creator without a full character definition</td></tr>\n<tr><td>ID only</td><td>the ID was seen but no usable definition might be saved</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Deleted / unavailable</td><td>public checks found unavailability; this doesn't restore the original bot</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Unknown / inconclusive</td><td>there isn't enough reliable evidence for a firm conclusion</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>if the Personality or Scenario is missing, that doesn't mean the original creator left it empty. it might just never have been collected.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"versions\">which archived version should you choose?</h2>\n<p>you might find multiple historical changes for one bot. that doesn't automatically mean every edit was a complete different version, and it doesn't mean all fields were saved equally well.</p>\n<ol><li>open the Archive record and compare the saved title, Greeting and other fields with what you remember.</li><li>in Recovery Lab, find <strong>Base recovery on</strong>. choose <strong>Latest archived evidence</strong> for the most recent saved state, or pick one of the dated older entries.</li><li>check the editor and its <strong>Archived Original</strong> badges after switching. the starting evidence can change with the selected date.</li><li>only start adding your own draft text after choosing your base. switching the version resets the editor from the selected archived evidence, so you should export an existing working copy first if you already edited it.</li></ol>\n<p>the date selector is built from the Archive's recorded field history. it isn't a time machine that reconstructs an unsaved full snapshot. an old field value is only as trustworthy as the historical evidence that was captured.</p>\n<h2 id=\"readiness\">understanding the Recovery evidence & readiness panel</h2>\n<p>after loading the bot, the page shows how many of its <strong>eight supported fields</strong> have some archived value. this is a presence check, not a score for quality or completeness.</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>What to check</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Name</td><td>is it the actual character's name or a default title?</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Title</td><td>does it describe the original premise, and is it the correct older version?</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Greeting</td><td>is the first playable message complete and in the original writing style?</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Personality</td><td>were the defining traits and relationships actually saved?</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Scenario</td><td>is the opening situation present, rather than inferred from a later chat?</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Example Dialogue</td><td>is this real example text or an empty field?</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Tags</td><td>are they the old recorded tags or tags currently selectable in SpicyChat?</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Image URL</td><td>does the link still return a picture and is it the right character?</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p><strong>8 of 8 does not mean 100% authentic or fully complete.</strong> some fields might contain short snippets or old values. likewise, 2 of 8 doesn't mean the recovery is useless if those two fields include a long Greeting and Personality.</p>\n<p>the panel also gives warnings about missing or weak evidence. use those warnings to decide what needs verification before you start generating guesses.</p>\n<h2 id=\"avatar-evidence\">recovering an archived picture</h2>\n<p>if a picture is available, the readiness area can show <strong>Download archived picture</strong>, <strong>Use archived picture URL</strong> and <strong>View image</strong>. they're different actions.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>View image:</strong> opens the currently referenced image so you can inspect it.</li>\n<li><strong>Download archived picture:</strong> tries to download the image file to your device.</li>\n<li><strong>Use archived picture URL:</strong> copies or selects the saved image reference for your recovery editor; an old external URL may stop working later.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>the saved picture might be a resized version rather than the original upload. if direct downloading fails because the host blocks it, try viewing the image in your browser and using the browser's own save controls where permitted.</p>\n<p>if there's no archived picture, don't invent one and label it original. you can pick a replacement you own or have permission to use when you create your new bot.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"save-chat\">4. save your old chat while you can</h2>\n<p>if the old conversation still opens, keep a personal backup before you change or delete anything. that chat may be the best evidence you have for how the character actually spoke.</p>\n<ol><li>use an available export tool such as <a href=\"#qol-install\">DragonScript QoL (detailed setup below)</a> or S.AI Toolkit, or copy accessible chat text manually.</li><li>check that the downloaded file contains real messages; don't trust a filename alone.</li><li>keep the original file unchanged. Recovery Lab accepts supported JSON/HTML transcripts, Markdown, plain text, and pasted conversations.</li><li>if an export won't load, try a smaller supported transcript or paste some text using the recovery page's text box.</li></ol>\n<p><strong>your private chat doesn't need to be publicly uploaded to the Archive.</strong> the guide says conversation parsing is handled locally in your browser. optional External AI is different: only use it if you're comfortable sending selected evidence to that provider. nothing can export a chat your account can no longer access.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"qol-install\">4a. install DragonScript QoL if you don't already have it</h2>\n<p>QoL is optional and independent of SpicyChat. You do not need it to search the Archive, and installing it cannot unlock a conversation your account can no longer access.</p>\n<ol><li>On a computer, install from the <a href=\"https://chromewebstore.google.com/detail/dragonscript-spicychat-qo/jdbhnaohfjnmkfpfddnjilmpaemkmabh\">Chrome Web Store</a> (Chrome/Brave/compatible Chromium) or <a href=\"https://addons.mozilla.org/en-US/firefox/addon/dragonscript-spicychat-qol-dev/\">Firefox Add-ons</a>. Confirm installation and permissions.</li><li>Open the browser Extensions menu, find SpicyChat QoL, reload SpicyChat, open QoL Settings from the extension popup and check the main switch.</li><li>On Android use the separate <a href=\"https://spicychatqol.drache.uk/android/\">QoL Android app</a> and its QoL Settings. Desktop is easier for large ZIPs and downloads.</li><li>For manual or Lite installation, verify Chat Export is included. See <a href=\"/qol/#install\">the full installation and update guide</a> for loading packages and checking versions.</li></ol>\n<h2 id=\"qol-settings\">4b. the exact QoL switches for recovery</h2>\n<p>These labels are from full QoL v0.2.97. In Settings use Find a setting, then enable the switches individually. Keep unrelated settings unchanged; you do not need any blocking or filtering tools.</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Find this exact setting</th><th>Recommended choice</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Show Copy / Export chat buttons on chat pages</strong></td><td>On — required for single-chat export.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Include available bot info</strong></td><td>On — accessible identifying data, not hidden definitions.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Include current Rulebook meter snapshot (only visible information, not full history)</strong></td><td>On if relevant.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Include Rulebook state (Story Moments / Story Time) from currently mounted chat status updates</strong></td><td>On if relevant; older unmounted state is not reconstructed.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Include OOC and directive notes</strong></td><td>On for the private original; review before sharing.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Include captured timestamps/model/generation details when available</strong></td><td>On — available provenance.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Number exported messages</strong></td><td>On — easier review.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Include avatar links in HTML/JSON exports</strong></td><td>Optional — links are not image files.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Include message image links when available</strong></td><td>Optional — links can expire.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Default format</strong></td><td>JSON backup for structured evidence. HTML archive is a readable companion.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Enable bulk research exports</strong></td><td>Optional — many bots/conversations or native Memories; not needed for one chat.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Add Export to bot profile pages</strong></td><td>On if the profile still exists; save public evidence separately.</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>Save the changes and reopen the chat. To preserve future public profiles and IDs, also enable the Local Bot Archive and opened-history settings in <a href=\"/qol/#archive-setup\">the full recovery checklist</a>. They cannot retroactively recover vanished content.</p>\n<h2 id=\"qol-export\">4c. export the actual conversation with QoL</h2>\n<ol><li>Open the actual conversation, not just the bot's public profile or the Chats list. Check the account and conversation are correct.</li><li>Use the QoL <strong>Export</strong> control on the chat page. The Chat export dialog automatically attempts to fetch full accessible active history through the API without rendering old messages.</li><li>Check the result and message count. SpicyChat's displayed count can include deleted or replaced messages; it need not match the active transcript. If incomplete, use <strong>Retry full chat fetch</strong>.</li><li>Set Format to <strong>JSON backup</strong>. Confirm Bot info, OOC/directives, Captured timestamps/model details, Message numbers and relevant Rulebook snapshot (at export) / Rulebook state (Story Moments / Story Time). Avatar and image links are optional.</li><li>Leave <strong>Export censored copy (original by default)</strong> off for the private original. Censoring only masks message text, not all sensitive information.</li><li>Click <strong>Download JSON</strong>. Optionally change Format to HTML archive and choose Clean transcript (or Match current chat appearance), then Download HTML. Copy places the selected format on the clipboard; Print / Save PDF is a reading copy, not preferred Recovery Lab input.</li><li>Open the downloaded file and check bot identity, earliest and latest messages, speaker names and expected content. Keep the original unchanged. Make a separate working copy to redact or shorten.</li></ol>\n<p>If the API retry fails, Advanced / Compatibility offers Refresh chat data (API) and <strong>Load full chat through page (very slow)</strong>. The latter mounts old messages and may consume substantial memory; use only as a last resort. Save drafts before reloading. You do not need to disable older-message folding to export normally.</p>\n<p>Single-chat export does not guarantee deleted messages, replaced replies, the full native Memory database or hidden Rulebook definitions. If a conversation is no longer accessible, use existing authorized evidence rather than trying to bypass access restrictions.</p>\n<p>Open <a href=\"https://spicychatarchive.drache.uk/recovery/\">Recovery Lab</a>, select the conversation JSON with its file picker, then review the detected speakers, message count and message preview before accepting it. Continue with <a href=\"#review-messages\">the import review</a> below.</p>\n<h2 id=\"qol-research\">4d. back up many bots and native Memories before you lose them</h2>\n<p>For one missing bot, use the previous method. For your accessible chat library enable <strong>Enable bulk research exports</strong>, then click <strong>Open research export in a separate tab</strong>.</p>\n<ol><li>Choose Recent bots to export (default 50) or All bots. Each bot can have multiple conversations.</li><li>Choose Reuse cached / update stale under Character metadata and click Start / Resume. Keep every downloaded ZIP part.</li><li>Full scan discovers all bots/conversations; Verify all checks older saved histories. Start / Resume is incremental, Scan bots discovers changes and Fresh rebuilds the selected archive from scratch. Do not discard earlier ZIPs or clear checkpoints merely to update the backup.</li><li>Extract the ZIP. Look under <code>characters/</code> for the bot and <code>conversations/</code> for the conversation. Keep conversation.json, every messages-NNNN.json chunk and available memories, story moments, Rulebook state and OOC records. These are evidence, not character cards.</li><li>Recovery Lab does not directly accept ZIP. Use single-chat JSON if possible. If you only have split research files, assemble a valid speaker-labeled transcript in order and review what the importer recognizes.</li></ol>\n<p>Do not publish a whole private research archive or send it to public support. The conversation file limit is 20 MB; create a smaller valid transcript if necessary. See <a href=\"/qol/#research\">the complete Research Export instructions</a> for all controls and backup behavior.</p>\n<h2 id=\"qol-saved-copies\">4e. check QoL for saved profile evidence</h2>\n<p>If the old chat is gone, a previously captured QoL profile may still exist. Search Bot Status Center in Settings, then review <strong>Saved Bot Copies / Recovery</strong> or <strong>Deleted / Unavailable Saved Bots</strong> by name, creator or ID. Save the available record; do not use Forget or clear storage.</p>\n<p>If the profile is still accessible, enable Add Export to bot profile pages, visit it and download a separate public JSON or HTML snapshot. For a bot you own, a real editor backup or native character card may be more useful. Public copies cannot reveal hidden fields. Keep dated versions separate.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"conversation-formats\">which conversation files does Recovery Lab accept?</h2>\n<p>the current Recovery Lab file picker accepts <strong>.json, .html, .htm, .txt and .md</strong>. it tries to recognize common QoL exports, S.AI Toolkit exports and other transcripts with actual chat messages.</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Format</th><th>What to expect</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>JSON</td><td>best when the export has recognizable message arrays, roles and speaker information</td></tr>\n<tr><td>HTML</td><td>works if the page contains actual recognizable chat messages or embedded message data</td></tr>\n<tr><td>TXT / Markdown</td><td>simple transcripts can work, especially with speaker names before each message</td></tr>\n<tr><td>pasted text</td><td>use <strong>Use pasted conversation</strong> if you don't have a compatible file</td></tr>\n<tr><td>ZIP</td><td>not listed as a direct import format in the current file picker; extract an appropriate transcript first</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p><strong>the current file upload limit is 20 MB per conversation file.</strong> if your export is bigger, make a smaller valid transcript before importing. do not just rename a ZIP to .json or split JSON text at a random character, because that usually breaks the structure.</p>\n<p>if a saved HTML page only contains menus, navigation or an empty chat container, it might not have exported actual messages. opening the file and checking for real chat text can save you a lot of confusion.</p>\n<h3>a simple text example that is easy to parse</h3>\n<div class=\"example-box\"><pre>Mira: I didn't lose the key. I put it somewhere safe.\nUser: Where?\nMira: That's the part I'm still working out.\n\nMira: *She checks her coat pocket again.*\nUser: You're kidding, right?</pre></div>\n<p>speaker-prefixed text is a practical fallback, not a guarantee that every possible transcript layout will parse perfectly. check the imported preview afterward.</p>\n<h2 id=\"review-messages\">don't skip the imported-message preview</h2>\n<p>after a transcript loads, the page summarizes the detected total, character messages, user messages, OOC/system messages and possible regenerations. there is also <strong>Review imported messages and identity</strong>.</p>\n<p>open that preview and check three things: are the speakers assigned correctly, does the character name match the bot you're recovering, and are the messages actually in a sensible order? a different speaker name can trigger a warning, which is worth investigating before using that transcript as evidence.</p>\n<p>the three message-selection switches matter too:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Use user messages as relationship/context evidence:</strong> enabled by default. this helps reconstruct what the character was responding to, but a user's statement isn't always established world canon.</li>\n<li><strong>Include OOC/system/directive messages:</strong> off by default. enable only when those instructions are genuinely relevant to the character you want to reconstruct.</li>\n<li><strong>Include possible regenerated/swiped replies:</strong> off by default. alternative responses may contradict the actual branch you played.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>if the speaker labels or story facts look wrong, use <strong>Clear conversation</strong> and import the correct transcript. changing the inclusion switches also invalidates draft suggestions that were made using the previous selection.</p>\n<h2 id=\"large-chat\">what happens with a very long chat?</h2>\n<p>being able to <em>import</em> a long conversation isn't the same as every line being sent to an AI. Recovery Lab keeps a message summary, but its reconstruction prompt uses a <strong>bounded sample</strong>: currently up to about 140 selected messages and 90,000 characters, with the beginning and end represented when there are many more messages.</p>\n<p>this matters if one important detail happened halfway through a massive RP. don't assume the AI saw it just because the import counter says thousands of messages. check the prompt preview or include the important evidence in your own reviewed instructions.</p>\n<p>i'd rather use a good, representative chat or a few genuinely useful scenes than dump a completely unrelated history into the reconstruction.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"recovery-lab\">5. rebuild the character in Recovery Lab</h2>\n<p>open <a href=\"https://spicychatarchive.drache.uk/recovery/\">Recovery Lab</a>. the controls have actual names, so here's the order i'd use them in:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Choose a bot.</strong> paste its bot ID or public profile URL and click <strong>Load archive evidence</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Choose the right version.</strong> if the Archive saved older versions, use <strong>Base recovery on</strong> to choose the one you want.</li>\n<li><strong>Check readiness.</strong> the panel shows which of the eight supported character fields have data. a present field isn't always complete.</li>\n<li><strong>Add conversation evidence.</strong> drop a supported export into the page or paste text and select <strong>Use pasted conversation</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Review message selection.</strong> choose whether user messages, OOC messages or regenerated messages should contribute. they aren't all equally reliable evidence of the character's original definition.</li>\n<li><strong>Fill gaps.</strong> write missing fields yourself, try <strong>Quick Draft (offline)</strong>, or review optional Browser AI / External AI suggestions. <strong>Expand</strong> proposes additions to existing fields.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>AI suggestions are suggestions, not recovered original text. if the Archive never saved a field, don't label a newly written version as the creator's exact original.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"offline-vs-ai\">all the ways to fill missing fields</h2>\n<p>Recovery Lab gives you several options, and they don't all send your data to the same place.</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Write it yourself</td><td>edit the recovered fields manually, using what you know</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Quick Draft (offline)</td><td>makes an evidence-guided local draft without an AI model</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Quick Expand (offline)</td><td>suggests extra text for an existing field, rather than replacing the archived text</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Browser AI</td><td>uses an optional on-device browser model if your browser supports it</td></tr>\n<tr><td>External AI</td><td>requires choosing an external endpoint and consenting to send selected evidence</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Copy prompt / Download prompt</td><td>lets you use the prepared instructions in a separate tool of your choice</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>the missing-field suggestion buttons mainly target <strong>Personality, Scenario and Example Dialogue</strong>. <strong>Expand</strong> is available for existing narrative fields, including Greeting. the Archived Original is still the reference; accepting a draft changes your editable working copy, not the Archive record.</p>\n<h3>Quick Draft versus writing it yourself</h3>\n<p>Quick Draft tries to assemble a simple scaffold from the saved title, Greeting, traits and selected conversation clues. it's handy for getting started, but it doesn't magically know the creator's lost definition. if there is too little evidence, it may say it can't make a draft.</p>\n<p>you can edit the proposal before choosing <strong>Use this draft</strong>, or discard it. if you edit it, that matters for the field's provenance.</p>\n<h3>Expand without overwriting the original</h3>\n<p>for saved text that needs more detail, Expand proposes an addition to the editable field. <strong>Append</strong> makes a User Modified copy. it doesn't rewrite the original archived evidence.</p>\n<p>if you only want the authentic wording, don't use Expand at all. a longer Personality isn't automatically a more faithful Personality.</p>\n<h2 id=\"browser-ai\">Browser AI: useful, but not available everywhere</h2>\n<p>Browser AI is an <strong>optional on-device model</strong>. whether it works depends on the browser, hardware, permissions and feature flags. the page checks whether it seems to be available, and some browsers may need to download a model first.</p>\n<p>if it says unavailable or blocked, the Archive cannot force the browser to support it. use Quick Draft / Quick Expand or manually write the missing field instead.</p>\n<p>even when Browser AI works, review the suggestion for invented relationships, facts, personality traits or ages. the browser model is helping write a new draft, not fetching lost private text.</p>\n<h2 id=\"external-ai\">External AI: know what you're sending</h2>\n<p>the optional <strong>Direct AI reconstruction</strong> panel accepts a chat-completions-compatible endpoint and model, plus a key when needed. using it is a separate choice from importing your chat locally.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>open <strong>Direct AI reconstruction (optional)</strong>.</li>\n<li>enter the endpoint and model, and an API key only if the provider requires one.</li>\n<li>check the <strong>destination and evidence preview</strong> shown by the page.</li>\n<li>read the consent checkbox: <strong>I understand selected evidence will be sent to this endpoint</strong>.</li>\n<li>only then choose <strong>Reconstruct with AI</strong>, if you're comfortable with that provider.</li>\n<li>review the result before applying any proposed text.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>the current implementation requires HTTPS for remote endpoints (HTTP is allowed only for localhost). endpoint and model may be remembered in your browser; the API key is kept only in the current page session, not as a saved Archive account credential.</p>\n<p><strong>the selected archived fields, instructions and sampled chat evidence can leave your browser when you use an external endpoint.</strong> don't paste a random URL from someone else's tutorial and assume it's private. never put an API key in the endpoint URL itself.</p>\n<p>if the Archive identifies sensitive or ambiguous source material, some automated suggestions are restricted to safer non-explicit character details. that is not a promise that all historical content or any output is appropriate to republish.</p>\n<h2 id=\"prompt-builder\">build a reconstruction prompt yourself</h2>\n<p>you don't have to connect an AI provider to use the prompt builder. after choosing <strong>Reconstruct Original</strong> or <strong>Make It Mine</strong>, you can add optional instructions and select <strong>Build reconstruction prompt</strong>.</p>\n<p>then use <strong>Copy prompt</strong> or <strong>Download prompt</strong>. the prompt labels archived fields, missing fields, the selected historical version and the conversation evidence. it asks the model to keep those distinctions in the output.</p>\n<div class=\"example-box\"><pre>Example optional instructions (written by you):\nKeep the archived Greeting and Personality unchanged.\nFor the missing Scenario, use only facts established\nin the recovered conversation. Don't invent a new\nrelationship, history or setting.</pre></div>\n<p>that's an example of something you could enter, not a claim that an AI will obey it perfectly. if you put private conversation evidence into an external service manually, the same privacy caveats apply.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"original-vs-adapted\">6. reconstruct the original or make your own version?</h2>\n<p>Recovery Lab gives you two approaches. <strong>Reconstruct Original</strong> sticks close to available archived evidence; <strong>Make It Mine</strong> is for an intentionally adapted copy. neither can restore hidden content.</p>\n<p>pay attention to the evidence labels:</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Label</th><th>meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Archived Original</td><td>text actually captured by the Archive</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Reconstructed</td><td>new or inferred text, not proven original</td></tr>\n<tr><td>User Modified</td><td>something you deliberately changed</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Missing</td><td>nothing was captured for that field</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>say the chat shows that a character is sarcastic. that's good evidence for writing a sarcastic Personality in your reconstruction. it doesn't prove the original creator used the exact sentence you just wrote.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"field-provenance\">what do the evidence labels actually mean?</h2>\n<p>you'll see badges beside the editable fields. they're there for a reason: <strong>Archived Original</strong> is not the same as <strong>Reconstructed</strong>, and neither should silently turn into a supposed original creator quote.</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Badge</th><th>How i'd treat it</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Archived Original</td><td>public text actually captured by Archive; keep it unchanged if fidelity is your goal</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Reconstructed</td><td>a suggestion based on evidence, not verified original wording</td></tr>\n<tr><td>User Modified</td><td>you edited or appended something to your working copy</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Missing</td><td>no archived value is present for that field</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>the editor includes <strong>Restore archived</strong> for fields with saved evidence, <strong>Restore reconstruction</strong> when an applicable draft exists, and <strong>Clear</strong>. those can help undo edits, but don't rely on the browser tab as your only backup—export your working copy before making big changes.</p>\n<p>an AI may give a low/medium/high <strong>confidence</strong> estimate for a reconstructed field. that's a description of the evidence support, not proof that the original creator wrote those words.</p>\n<h2 id=\"worked-example\">a full example: rebuilding Mira the warehouse guard</h2>\n<p>here's a <strong>completely fictional example</strong> to show the difference between saved evidence and a new reconstruction:</p>\n<div class=\"example-box\"><pre>What Archive saved:\nName: Mira Vale\nTitle: The Guard Who Lost the Key\nGreeting: *Mira shuts the gate.* \"You're out late.\"\nPersonality: [NOT ARCHIVED]\nScenario: [NOT ARCHIVED]\n\nWhat the old conversation shows:\nMira is suspicious at first.\nLater she admits losing a warehouse key.\nShe eventually apologizes to the user.\n\nWhat you can honestly reconstruct:\nPersonality: Mira is initially cautious and proud.\nShe finds it difficult to admit mistakes, but may\napologize after she trusts someone.\n\nScenario: A guard at a locked warehouse meets\n{{user}} at night.\n\nThese are INFERRED DRAFTS, not the creator's text.</pre></div>\n<p>now compare that to something like <em>“Mira has always secretly been a royal assassin”</em>. if your Archive record and conversation never supported it, that's a new creative choice. use Make It Mine if you want it, but don't call it recovered lore.</p>\n<p>this is also why i'm careful with first encounters and relationship status. something that happened 300 chat messages later isn't necessarily how the bot was configured to behave in its opening Scenario.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"new-bot\">7. export the draft and make a new bot</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>review the eight fields: <strong>name, title, Greeting, Personality, Scenario, Example Dialogue, tags and picture</strong>.</li>\n<li>download a <strong>recovery package</strong> so you have a local backup. use <strong>Copy fields</strong> for manual creation.</li>\n<li>open SpicyChat's <strong>Create → Chatbot</strong> editor and place the reviewed information into its matching fields.</li>\n<li>if you use a JSON or PNG import, check that it is a supported <em>native character-card</em> format. Archive recovery packages aren't automatically native SpicyChat imports. our <a href=\"/bots/importing-characters/\">import guide</a> covers that difference.</li>\n<li>only use images and content you have permission to reuse; keep private recreations private when appropriate. don't use recovery to evade moderation.</li>\n<li>save the new character and test it in a new chat.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"guide-callout\"><strong>it will be a new bot.</strong> it gets a new ID. your original conversation history, ratings, native memories and private Rulebook values aren't automatically moved over.</div>\n<p>and check the download before sharing it. recovery packages exclude raw chat-message text, but the reconstructed <em>fields</em> may include private things taken from your conversation.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"export-formats\">which export button should you use?</h2>\n<p>the current <strong>Edit & export</strong> area has several options. they have different jobs, so please don't assume every download is a native SpicyChat character import.</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Button</th><th>What's inside / best use</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Download recovery package</td><td>Recovery Lab's own JSON, including the fields, original bot ID, chosen base version, provenance, confidence and a conversation summary</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Download character-card JSON</td><td>Character Card V2-style data for compatible card tools; the image is referenced by URL</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Download field JSON</td><td>fields plus provenance/confidence, useful for inspecting or transferring recovered text</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Copy fields</td><td>copies readable values with their provenance labels, handy for manually creating a bot</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Download image character card (PNG)</td><td>tries to create an image-bearing Character Card V2 PNG with embedded character data</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Preview export</td><td>shows the character-card structure before you download it</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>the recovery package contains a <strong>conversation summary, not the raw conversation messages</strong>. the fields themselves can still reveal private details that were inferred from your chat, so open the downloaded file before sharing it.</p>\n<p>the Character Card V2 export may include legacy description information in an extension, and its standard Description field may be empty if the original data didn't contain one. that's not the same as missing the SpicyChat Personality field.</p>\n<h2 id=\"png-troubleshooting\">why did the character-card PNG fail?</h2>\n<p>PNG export has to fetch the picture and sometimes convert it in the browser. it can fail even if the character text is perfectly fine.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>No image URL:</strong> choose a picture first or use character-card JSON without an embedded image.</li>\n<li><strong>Expired or unavailable picture:</strong> the old host may no longer return the file.</li>\n<li><strong>Cross-origin restrictions (CORS):</strong> the browser may refuse to let the Archive read and package an external image.</li>\n<li><strong>Large or unsupported conversion:</strong> the current browser image-fetch code rejects pictures over 25 MB and limits very large PNG conversions.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>if that happens, download <strong>character-card JSON</strong> and save the image separately if you can. using an image URL in JSON is not the same as embedding the picture in a PNG.</p>\n<h2 id=\"create-and-test\">before you publish the replacement</h2>\n<p>use the live SpicyChat editor as the final check. a historically saved tag might no longer be selectable, and old fields don't always map one-to-one onto the current creator.</p>\n<ol><li>compare the imported or copied text with the archived evidence one final time.</li><li>review the title and Greeting to make sure they don't force the new player to act or feel something.</li><li>verify the current Persona/POV assumptions and relationship setup.</li><li>check the image's permission and whether it still loads.</li><li>choose appropriate privacy/visibility settings and valid current tags.</li><li>save a separate copy, start a new chat and test a few different replies.</li></ol>\n<p>the reconstructed bot does not inherit old chat messages, ratings, saves, links, native memories, or private Rulebook state. a new ID is expected, not a sign that Recovery Lab failed.</p>\n<p>if you only want a personal recreation, keeping it Private may be the least confusing option. do not republish other people's hidden definitions or content to bypass platform moderation.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"books\">8. what about missing Lorebooks and Rulebooks?</h2>\n<p>try <a href=\"https://spicychatarchive.drache.uk/deleted-lorebooks/\">Deleted Lorebooks</a> or <a href=\"https://spicychatarchive.drache.uk/deleted-rulebooks/\">Deleted Rulebooks</a>, along with the public Lorebook/Rulebook directories. old listings, covers and attachment links can exist without a full saved book.</p>\n<p>the Archive can't recover a private entry or hidden Rulebook definition just because a public bot referred to it. for building your own new content, use the <a href=\"/lorebook/basics/\">Lorebook</a> or <a href=\"/rulebook/basics/\">Rulebook</a> guides.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"lorebook-rulebook-limits\">Lorebooks, Rulebooks and attached-bot relationships</h2>\n<p>a bot might have had a Lorebook, a Rulebook, or both. if those were public and the Archive recorded them, their titles, links, saved public details and history may help you recreate a broader world.</p>\n<p>but <strong>a public listing or attachment link is not the full book</strong>. a Lorebook cover doesn't prove the entry text was saved, and a Rulebook listing doesn't mean every hidden trigger or private meter configuration can be reconstructed.</p>\n<p>the Archive's <a href=\"https://spicychatarchive.drache.uk/lorebooks/\">Public Lorebooks</a>, <a href=\"https://spicychatarchive.drache.uk/rulebooks/\">Public Rulebooks</a>, and their deleted-listing pages are useful for looking for related records. if you have a creator-owned file backup, that's generally better evidence than guessing from the bot's chat.</p>\n<p>remember to treat the three resources separately: <strong>character</strong> (Name, Greeting, Personality...), <strong>Lorebook</strong> (conditional reference facts), and <strong>Rulebook</strong> (configured state, meters and triggers). recovering one does not automatically recreate the other two.</p>\n<h2 id=\"privacy-check\">a quick privacy check before using AI or sharing files</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>is the bot ID public? then sharing the <strong>public bot link</strong> is usually enough for someone to help find it.</li>\n<li>does your screenshot show private messages, profile details or tokens? crop or redact those before asking for help.</li>\n<li>did you upload a transcript into Recovery Lab? the basic parser is local, but using <strong>External AI</strong> or manually pasting a prompt into another service can send selected content to that service.</li>\n<li>did the downloaded recovery package contain inferred private facts? check the <strong>reconstructed fields</strong>, not just whether it contains the raw messages.</li>\n<li>are you recreating somebody else's bot? respect the creator's rights and current SpicyChat rules; private use is different from publishing somebody else's definition.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>you can ask for troubleshooting help without posting a full transcript. a redacted error, bot UUID, file type and a short description often tell people what they need to know.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"troubleshooting\">9. still stuck?</h2>\n<details class=\"recovery-question\"><summary>nothing comes up in search</summary><p>try the exact ID, title variants, creator, Public bots and historical search. remove filters. the Archive may only have a partial record or none at all.</p></details>\n<details class=\"recovery-question\"><summary>the mobile app won't share the link</summary><p>try the browser version, old bookmarks or an existing chat URL. send just the public bot ID when asking for help.</p></details>\n<details class=\"recovery-question\"><summary>the Archive only has a Greeting or picture</summary><p>that's all that was captured. supplement it with your own conversation or notes and mark new material as reconstructed.</p></details>\n<details class=\"recovery-question\"><summary>my conversation export doesn't work</summary><p>open the file and confirm it contains actual messages. try a supported format or paste a small section into Recovery Lab.</p></details>\n<details class=\"recovery-question\"><summary>will my old bot and chat come back?</summary><p>not through the Archive. you can make a separate new character, but its ID and conversation are new.</p></details>\n<details class=\"recovery-question\"><summary>where can i ask for help?</summary><p>use the <a href=\"https://discord.gg/XTMdWvuVSU\">QoL Discord</a> or <a href=\"https://github.com/drachescript/spicychat-archive/issues\">Archive GitHub Issues</a>. include the public bot ID and a short explanation, not credentials or private exports.</p></details>\n\n<h2 id=\"deep-troubleshooting\">more fixes for less obvious problems</h2>\n<details class=\"recovery-question\"><summary>Recovery Lab says the UUID is invalid</summary><p>check that you're pasting an actual 36-character hyphenated UUID, preferably from a public bot link. don't use the chat ID, creator username or an image URL. you can paste the full SpicyChat bot URL if it contains the UUID.</p></details>\n<details class=\"recovery-question\"><summary>the record loads, but the fields are mostly empty</summary><p>look for Search entry only or ID only. the Archive may never have saved the full definition. try another recorded date, a creator-owned backup, or your own chat evidence. a missing field is not proof it was blank in SpicyChat.</p></details>\n<details class=\"recovery-question\"><summary>the site finds an older version, but it isn't the one i remember</summary><p>try another option under Base recovery on. compare title, Greeting and dates. old versions depend on what was actually captured, and selecting another base refreshes the working fields.</p></details>\n<details class=\"recovery-question\"><summary>my file is bigger than 20 MB</summary><p>the current browser file picker limits conversation imports to 20 MB. create a smaller valid transcript, for example by exporting fewer messages or a single conversation, or paste a representative excerpt. don't simply rename a ZIP or cut a JSON file in half.</p></details>\n<details class=\"recovery-question\"><summary>the exported HTML opens, but Recovery Lab finds no messages</summary><p>open the file and check whether it really contains chat content. a browser's “Save page” feature might save only the interface. try a tool-generated conversation export or a simple speaker-labeled text transcript.</p></details>\n<details class=\"recovery-question\"><summary>the wrong character's messages appeared</summary><p>open Review imported messages and identity and check the speaker labels. if the export is from another bot, use Clear conversation and import the right one before generating drafts.</p></details>\n<details class=\"recovery-question\"><summary>the AI keeps inventing extra traits</summary><p>prefer Reconstruct Original, compare against archived fields, and add narrow instructions referencing the exact evidence. use Quick Draft or write missing fields manually. anything unsupported is still invented, however convincing it sounds.</p></details>\n<details class=\"recovery-question\"><summary>Browser AI is unavailable</summary><p>that depends on your browser and device. Quick Draft and Quick Expand work without a model. you can also copy the prompt to a tool you choose, taking care with private content.</p></details>\n<details class=\"recovery-question\"><summary>an external AI endpoint fails</summary><p>check HTTPS, endpoint compatibility, model name, your provider key and consent checkbox. only use endpoints you trust. the Archive cannot fix a provider's network or billing issue.</p></details>\n<details class=\"recovery-question\"><summary>the image-download or PNG button fails</summary><p>try View image. for character cards, use JSON and save the picture separately. the browser may be blocked by CORS, a missing URL or a conversion limit.</p></details>\n<details class=\"recovery-question\"><summary>the download says it succeeded, but i can't find a file</summary><p>check your browser's Downloads list and downloads folder, then inspect the file size and open it. browser download permissions, file pickers or blocked downloads can interrupt saves without changing the original archived data.</p></details>\n<h2 id=\"checklist\">the complete recovery checklist</h2>\n<div class=\"example-box\"><pre>[ ] Found the exact public bot ID, or searched by name/creator\n[ ] Confirmed the right character and Archive record\n[ ] Checked whether older versions exist\n[ ] Saved any accessible old conversation\n[ ] Checked the export contains real messages\n[ ] Selected the intended Base recovery on version\n[ ] Reviewed all eight archived fields and their labels\n[ ] Imported a supported conversation (20 MB or less)\n[ ] Checked speaker identity and message-selection options\n[ ] Chose Reconstruct Original or Make It Mine\n[ ] Reviewed every draft, expansion and AI-suggested detail\n[ ] Saved a recovery package or readable field backup\n[ ] Checked Character Card JSON/PNG compatibility\n[ ] Checked the picture, tags and current SpicyChat rules\n[ ] Created and tested the new bot\n[ ] Kept original evidence private and safely backed up</pre></div>\n<p>you don't have to fill every box. if the Archive never saved the bot and you have no conversation, you may not have enough evidence for a faithful copy. that's better to say clearly than to promise a fake “100% recovery.”</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"useful-links\">quick links</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://spicychatarchive.drache.uk/guide/\">Original Archive for Dummies</a> — source walkthrough.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://spicychatarchive.drache.uk/search/\">Archive Search</a> — names and historical text.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://spicychatarchive.drache.uk/deleted/\">Deleted bots</a> — unavailable records.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://spicychatarchive.drache.uk/recovery/\">Recovery Lab</a> — use evidence and build a new character.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/archive/\">Full Archive guide</a> — all the other features.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><small>checked November 24, 2026 against the uploaded Archive for Dummies HTML and <a href=\"https://github.com/drachescript/spicychat-archive/blob/main/guide/index.html\">Archive's current source page</a>. the Archive is an independent historical resource, not an official SpicyChat restoration service.</small></p>"
  }
}
);
